ally responsible way  if there has ever been a moment to be  thinking about how to prevent pandemics  before they start and do it on a global  scale with global cooperation and  collaboration the moment is now the  advisory commission's ultimate goal is  to keep science front and center  creating a clear path to identifying  future diseases too meetings will be  confidential to cut out political noise  however one obstacle to overcome it  can't compel countries to participate we  need the cooperation of that member  state to carry out any future missions  but without a global effort work can  grind to a halt and disease experts say  the clock is ticking viral outbreaks are  common and tracing origins is a  monumental task reconstructing events  after the fact is always much more  difficult and the further away you get  from that event the harder it is to  collect the data now attempting to tie  up loose threads on a virus that's still  so unknown opportunity arises to learn  from past mistakes  reggie shakini global news washington  do big cities still mean bigger earnings  ahead why more canadians are cashing out  of that belief  there is a long-held belief that living  in bigger cities like toronto or  vancouver could mean access to higher  wages but recent research by a canadian  nobel laureate suggests the two don't  necessarily go hand in hand especially  when you factor in skyrocketing housing  prices and as ann gaviola explains it's  prompting more workers to go rural  dave battersby grew up in a small town  and he always knew he wanted to go back  and when kovitt hit he and his family  moved from toronto to coburg it was  extremely scary to pick up and move  during the pandemic a hybrid work  arrangement means he makes the hour and  a half long commute to toronto twice a  week he says small town life hasn't  dented his earnings but his living costs  are lower and they're in a much bigger  home mortgage-free we only had about a  thousand square feet there and when we  moved we moved to about 5000 square feet  the home we moved to  has a pool the kids have been enjoying  it every day since since we've moved  here throughout the summer months  findings released this week by  researchers at the university of  california at berkeley examined u.s data  between 2010 and 2018. it found that the  high cost of housing in big cities  erodes the advantage of a bigger salary  meaning you may benefit from more and  better amenities in an urban center but  you're typically not getting further  ahead financially along comes  a pandemic and this uh this hard shift  to working remotely businesses like mine  just transformed overnight and and  our staff that had always come into the  office were working from home the exodus  from downtown coors began about five  years ago as more people moved from  expensive urban centers to the suburbs  and rural areas with kovic giving rise  to flexible and hybrid work models royal  lepage highlights a big pickup in people  moving from ontario to the atlantic  provinces during the pandemic as well as  a migration from the lower mainland to  northern bc and parts of alberta  including calgary it's hard to find good  people and if you find the worker you  want that is not close to where your  office is you're going to be pretty  flexible about letting them work with  you despite rumblings this summer about  some big tech firms in the u.s like  facebook and google reducing pay for  workers who live in less expensive  cities economist linda nazareth says  that won't fly in this tight labor  market in an email to global news google  canada says outside the u.s each country  has a single compensation category so  there are no differences in pay whether  you're a fully remote or in-office  employee battersby says there are  trade-offs living in a smaller community  but they're worth it couldn't be happier  about our decision to move  it has really changed the lifestyle for  our family ann gaviola global news 